# MEMORY.md — universal context (example)

> Your assistant's long-term memory. A nightly rollup promotes durable facts here
> from daily notes — you don't usually edit it by hand. Keep it **lean**: durable
> preferences, patterns, and lessons only. **Never** store point-in-time snapshots
> (counts, "currently in X", today's numbers) here — they decay; pull those live.
>
> Copy this to `MEMORY.md` and make it yours.

## Top Rules (read first, applied every turn)

- Reply language: `<your default>`. Switch only when explicitly asked.
- Memory, durable files, and internal notes are always written in `<language>`.
- Don't cite snapshot data from this file as if it were live — hit the real source.

## About the owner

- `<name, role, what you're building — the durable stuff that won't change weekly>`
- Prefers concise, high-signal answers. Ship-first.

## Preferences

- Temperature in Celsius. Times in `<timezone>`.
- `<formatting rules, tooling conventions, how you like things done>`

## Lessons Learned

> One line each — mistakes the assistant has made before and should not repeat.

- e.g. "Don't auto-add *optional* calendar invites — only confirmed events."
- e.g. "Read the email body before classifying financial-service senders."
- e.g. "Never put a secret value into a chat message or a committed file."
